WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:10.400
The American working class faces a
host of challenges, from stagnant family incomes

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:14.120
to dramatic increases in so-called
deaths of despair — from drugs,

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:19.050
alcohol, and suicide. But perhaps none is
greater than the decline of the married,

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:23.340
two-parent home. Unlike 50 years ago,
stable families are longer than norm

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:27.689
in working-class America. This takes a toll
on working-class kids growing up in a

00:00:27.689 --> 00:00:30.800
home without both parents — they
are more likely to fail in school,

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:35.720
end up pregnant as teens, or land in jail.
It's also hard for their parents, who are

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:39.629
more likely to fall prey to poverty,
depression, and even deaths of despair

00:00:39.629 --> 00:00:43.640
if they've been touched by
family breakdown. So what to do?

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:48.300
There are policy steps — like expanding wage
subsidies — that the US can take to strengthen

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:53.730
the economic foundations of working-class life.
But the most important change just might

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:59.280
be cultural: men and women in working-class
America must see that their futures — and the

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:03.180
future of their children — depend on
renewing stable marriage for themselves,

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:06.460
their friends, and their communities.

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:12.080
What do you think family breakdown has to do with the state of working-class America?

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:13.860
Let us know in your comments.

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:18.420
Also, let us know what other topics you'd like
our scholars to cover in 60 seconds

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:24.160
and be sure to LIKE and subscribe for more
research and videos from AEI.

